Let $\mathrm{Ar}$ denote the 2,5-dimethoxyphenyl group. **A is the doubly C-alkylated <beta-ketoester>**
$$
\boxed{\mathrm{EtO-C(=O)-C(CH_2CH_2Ar)(CH_2CH_2CH{=}CH_2)-C(=O)-CH_2-O-tBu}}.
$$
The central <carbon> has no <hydrogen> after the second step. The <alkene>, <ester> and tert-butyl <ether> remain intact.

<carbonate> reversibly removes a <proton> from the <methylene group> between the two <carbonyl groups>. The resulting resonance-stabilized <enolate> attacks the primary iodoethyl <carbon> of the aryl-containing reagent by an $S_\mathrm N2$ displacement, releasing <iodide>. The monoalkylated intermediate still has one <proton> at the same activated <carbon>. A second <deprotonation> followed by $S_\mathrm N2$ attack on the primary <bromide> introduces the but-3-en-1-yl group.

The beta-dicarbonyl position is much more acidic than the <methylene group> next to the <ketone> and <ether> at the other end, explaining the alkylation site. Counting the retained <carbon> skeleton gives $\mathrm{C_{24}H_{36}O_6}$, including the two <carbonyl groups> and one terminal <alkene>. The second panel of the original product drawing specifies this connectivity.

= ii
{parent=b}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

**The new ring is formed by intramolecular Friedel-Crafts addition to the <ketone>.** <titanium tetrachloride> coordinates the <ketone> <oxygen> of the cyclic <beta-ketoester>, increasing electrophilicity at that <carbonyl> <carbon>. The nearby electron-rich aryl ring attacks this activated <ketone> at the available position ortho to its ethyl tether.

The electron-flow sequence is: an aromatic $\pi$ bond forms the new aryl–carbonyl-carbon bond, the <ketone> $\pi$ bond moves onto <oxygen>, and loss of the aromatic <proton> restores <aromaticity>. The two-carbon tether and the shared quaternary <carbon> make the newly closed carbocycle six-membered. Hydrolytic work-up converts the titanium-bound <alkoxide> into the tertiary <alcohol>.

The <lactone> <carbonyl> survives. This is an addition to the <ketone> with retention of its <oxygen> as OH, rather than an acyl substitution that removes that <oxygen>. The pendant terminal <alkene> is not involved. These bond changes account for the fused <aromatic ring> system and the <alcohol> in C without needing to assign the new <stereochemistry>.

