Number the <enone> <carbonyl> <carbon> C1, the adjacent <alkene> <carbon> C2 and the ethoxy-bearing <alkene> <carbon> C3. In the **bromine-first pathway**, the enol-ether-like $\mathrm{C2{=}C3-OEt}$ unit attacks $\mathrm{Br_2}$ at C2, breaking the Br–Br bond. The intermediate has a new C2–Br bond and positive charge at C3, stabilized as an oxonium <chemical resonance> contributor by donation from the ethoxy <oxygen>.

<triethylamine> removes the remaining C2 <hydrogen>. Its C–H bonding pair reforms C2=C3 and neutralizes the oxonium system. The result is **replacement of the vinylic C2–H by C2–Br**, with the original <enone> and ethoxy group retained. If <bromide> first captures the cationic intermediate, the equivalent vicinal dibromide can eliminate HBr to reach the same product.

The ethoxy group makes this <alkene> sufficiently electron-rich for the electrophilic-addition description. It is important to place <bromine> at C2 and restore the <double bond>, rather than stop at a saturated addition product.

= ii
{parent=a}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For an **amine-first pathway**, use <triethylamine> as a <nucleophile> in a reversible <conjugate addition>. Its <lone pair> attacks C3 of the <enone>; <electron> shifts through C2 and the <carbonyl> give a zwitterionic <enolate> with a C3–$\mathrm{NEt_3^+}$ bond.

The C2 nucleophilic site of that <enolate> attacks $\mathrm{Br_2}$, installing C2–Br and releasing <bromide>. Removal of C2–H by base then reforms C2=C3 while the C3–N bond breaks back to neutral <triethylamine>. This addition–bromination–elimination sequence gives the same **C2-brominated <enone>** and regenerates the nucleophilic amine, with another base equivalent accepting the eliminated <proton>.

These are alternative formal sequences requested by the question, not a kinetic determination that one must dominate under unspecified conditions. In particular, the amine-first sequence does not require <triethylamine> to generate a high concentration of a vinylic <carbanion> by direct <deprotonation>.

= b
{parent=B2}
