= Solution
{parent=d}

<copper>(I) converts the methyl <grignard reagent> into a softer organocopper <nucleophile>. Methyl undergoes **1,4 addition** to the beta <carbon> of the cycloheptenone, giving a metal <enolate> rather than direct 1,2 addition to the <ketone> <carbonyl>.

The <carbon> nucleophilic site of this <enolate>, at the alpha <carbon>, attacks the <aldehyde> <carbonyl> in the second reagent. Its C=O $\pi$ <electrons> move to <oxygen>; <protonation> of the resulting <alkoxide> gives the beta-hydroxy <ketone> side chain. The cyclic <acetal> is a protected <ketone> and survives these nucleophilic conditions.

The beta <methyl group> shields one face of the cyclic <enolate>. Aldol bond formation from the less crowded opposite face places the new alpha side chain **trans to the beta <methyl group>**. Thus the two starred ring centers have an anti relationship; the two mirror-related products are possible from achiral starting materials and reagents. This argument does not assign the separate, unstarred <alcohol> center, for which the question gives no specified configuration.

