= Solution
{parent=b}

**B is the major product: the newly formed OH is the dashed bond in the printed zigzag structure.** Retain the existing solid-wedge OH and the configuration at the carbamate-bearing <carbon>.

<zinc> coordinates the <ketone> <oxygen> and the adjacent hydroxyl <oxygen>, forming a five-membered O–Zn–O chelate. This constrains rotation around the <carbonyl>–alpha-carbon bond. In the favored chelated <conformation> the bulky $\mathrm{CH(NHCO_2R')CH_2OR}$ group shields one face of the <ketone>, while the alpha <hydrogen> leaves the other more accessible. <hydride> attacks from that less crowded face, the <carbonyl> $\pi$ bond opens onto <oxygen>, and aqueous work-up gives the second <alcohol>.

Mapping this **<Cram chelation model>** back onto the printed zigzag gives B, not A. Merely placing a new OH “on the same side” of an unrotated page drawing is not a valid <chelation> argument: the reactive chelate and that drawing use different <conformations>. Zinc-borohydride reductions of alpha-hydroxy <ketones> are experimentally known to favor the corresponding anti-1,2-diol family under <chelation> control.<Stereoselective Reduction of 2-Hydroxy Ketones towards syn- and anti-1,2-Diols by S. M. Husain and others>[Abstract, chelation-controlled zinc-borohydride reductions]{ref}

The reagent combination can generate <zinc borohydride> and related zinc-associated reducing species. The stereochemical justification is coordination-controlled facial selectivity; it does not require identifying a unique solution aggregate from the reagent list. The quoted yield is not itself a measurement of a 95:5 diastereomeric ratio.

= c
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The first step is a **vinylogous <aldol condensation> at C5 of the unsaturated <lactam>**. Number <nitrogen> N1, the <lactam> <carbonyl> C2, and then the methoxy-substituted <alkene> C3=C4 and the <methylene group> C5.

<hydroxide> reversibly removes a C5 <proton>. The resulting <vinylogous enolate> is delocalized through C5–C4–C3–C2–O: it is not necessary to create an unstabilized isolated <carbanion> beside <nitrogen>. Its C5 nucleophilic contributor attacks acetone. <protonation> gives a C5-linked tertiary <alcohol>, $\mathrm{C5-C(OH)(Me)_2}$. Further removal of the remaining C5 <hydrogen> and elimination through an aldol-dehydration sequence forms **C5=C(Me)2**, leaving the original methoxy <enol ether> in place.

In the second step, **aqueous acid hydrolyses that <enol ether> to a <ketone> at C4**. <protonation> at C3 gives an oxygen-stabilized cation at C4. <water> attacks C4, <proton transfers> form a hemiacetal-like intermediate, and loss of <methanol> followed by <deprotonation> produces C4=O and the C3 <methylene group>.

The <lactam> <carbonyl> and N–H group remain, as does the exocyclic isopropylidene group. This accounts for replacement of the C3=C4–OMe unit by C3H2–C4=O without inserting a new <oxygen> into the ring skeleton.

= d
{parent=B4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The hydroxyl group first binds <aluminium>, normally by <deprotonation> to an <aluminium> <alkoxide> with release of <hydrogen>. Coordination also brings <aluminium> close to the <alkyne>. **<directed trans-hydroalumination>** then puts <hydride> on the <alkyne> <carbon> proximal to CH2O and <aluminium> on the distal <carbon>.

One useful mechanistic description has external <hydride> attacking the coordinated <alkyne> opposite the aluminium-containing face. The resulting <vinylaluminate> is stabilized by a five-membered O–Al–C–C–CH2 chelate. This is not the ordinary undirected syn addition of a neutral dialkylaluminium <hydride>. Propargylic-alcohol-directed trans-hydroalumination with <Red-Al> gives <vinylaluminate> intermediates that can be intercepted by <electrophiles>.<trans-Hydroalumination and alkylation by Neil F. Langille and Timothy F. Jamison>[Abstract, stereoselective vinylalanate formation from propargylic alcohols]{ref}

<iodine> replaces the distal C–Al bond with C–I without scrambling the <alkene> geometry; aqueous work-up releases the <alcohol>. <hydride> and <iodine> are consequently **trans** across the <alkene>, while <iodine> and the CH2OH group are on the same side.

By <Cahn-Ingold-Prelog priority rules> priority the product is therefore **$Z$**: CH2OH outranks H at one <alkene> <carbon> and I outranks CH2R' at the other. Describing the two <carbon> chains as trans would not make this an $E$ <alkene>, because the <iodine> changes which substituent has higher priority.

